const sysengCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Systems engineering is the discipline of wholes', note: 'Components optimised separately produce systems that fail together. SE owns the interfaces, the requirements and the integration — the stuff that lives between the disciplines (an aeroplane is 4 million parts and a million conversations).' },
    { math: '2 · Requirements: the contract with reality', note: 'Elicit, write unambiguously, verify. The V-model: requirements → design → build, mirrored by verification → integration → validation. Most project failures are requirement failures discovered late (the software lesson\'s spec discipline, industrialised).' },
    { math: '3 · Interfaces and configuration control', note: 'Every interface (mechanical, electrical, data, human) is a chance for two teams to assume different things. Configuration management freezes the "as-designed" truth; change control prices every modification honestly — the unsung hero of projects that land.' },
    { math: '4 · Trade studies and the systems view', note: 'Every design decision is a trade (mass vs range, cost vs reliability — the Ashby lesson at system scale). The systems engineer models the trades, because no single discipline sees the whole curve — and "optimise the system, not the component" is the discipline\'s first commandment.' },
  ],
};

